{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_21-49-09-omnipaxos-real-run/draft && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -c \\\"\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('update.json'))\nprint(list(d.keys()))\nprint(json.dumps(d, indent=1))\n\\\" | head -200\"","aggregated_output":"['rationale', 'interpretations']\n{\n \"rationale\": \"Correcting the current account of this question, because it carried the sentence I revised in knowledge last turn: that the existing runtime test only asserts a non-zero index and a non-empty suffix. Two of its scenarios assert the value at the returned index (one refills an index after a lost entry and asserts the index holds the refill, the other asserts each returned index holds the entry submitted to it, with the message 'wrong-index assignment'), which means the repository's own expectations already run the way the finding needs, rather than only the example's documentation. I re-file the account with that integrated and with the two test expectations cited, since an Interpretation is the current explanation and leaving a superseded sentence in it would misstate the basis; the two mechanisms, their conditions, the counterevidence and the limits are otherwise as recorded and unchanged.\",\n \"interpretations\": [\n  {\n   \"investigation_id\": \"7428c4a1533941c1bcd9707354ba9245-question-0\",\n   \"experiment_id\": \"23140bc3cd9a47d0b58d6d9ef6c1179f-experiment\",\n   \"source_ids\": [\n    \"actor_drain_notifiers\",\n    \"actor_record_assignment\",\n    \"actor_pending_assigned_field\",\n    \"actor_append_tracked\",\n    \"handle_append_notify_contract\",\n    \"event_superseded_contract\",\n    \"actor_run_tick_branch\",\n    \"runtime_default_tick\",\n    \"example_durability_caller\",\n    \"runtime_test_expectation\",\n    \"runtime_test_loss_refill_expectation\",\n    \"runtime_test_wrong_index_expectation\",\n    \"core_flush_batch_follower\",\n    \"core_flush_batch_tick\",\n    \"core_accsync_valid_entries\"\n   ],\n   \"executions\": {\n    \"257dc12155634b38ade04579abd4b62f\": [\n     1\n    ],\n    \"7b535abd3cd24baa927f2ac8f7d8d597\": [\n     1\n    ],\n    \"10db1b327b784fb48711dec936afc73d\": [\n     1,\n     3,\n     5\n    ],\n    \"84fd61ba7e8240c8aff44e0559fa7ec4\": [\n     1\n    ],\n    \"caf6afe02aa54cfb907f4ed395281e1c\": [\n     1\n    ],\n    \"c6340d5867814bad9fcff41853a3bca8\": [\n     1\n    ]\n   },\n   \"conclusion\": \"violated\",\n   \"defect_claim\": \"asserted\",\n   \"joint_history\": \"One fixed experiment and its successive revisions on one construction: the original three-case probe, the tick-period sweep, the revision adding the origin's promise and phase measurements, the revision parameterising the follower's flush deadline, and the revision parameterising it for the parameterised case as well. Scenario steps, batch size, election and resend tick counts and the measured quantities are unchanged across them; only the tick period, the flush deadline, the poll flag and read-only measurements differ, so the six configurations read as one comparison.\",\n   \"rationale\": \"Current account of both mechanisms this question turned up in the actor layer's `append_notify` completion path, with the repo's own expectations as the basis for what the resolution is supposed to establish. First, the resolution arms: `drain_notifiers` answers `Ok(assigned_idx)` as soon as the local decided index reaches the index recorded at assignment, before evaluating the ballot-change arm and without comparing the caller's entry with what occupies that index, and it runs only from the actor's tick branch. A write accepted only by the leader it was assigned to, then dropped by a leader change, therefore resolves `Ok` with an index holding a different entry; the same lost-entry state resolves the documented `Superseded` instead when a tick falls between the origin's promise change and its decided-index advance, and the two arms are separated by the tick period against the re-synchronisation round trip: with a ~2 ms round trip the sweep held at 2 and 4 ms ticks and violated at 8, 16, 40 and 80 ms, and the crate's default tick period is 10 ms. Second, the assignment mapping: deferred writes are tracked as one placeholder per buffered entry, one placeholder is consumed per unit of accepted-index advance, and a placeholder is discarded while still consuming its advance when its buffered ballot no longer matches the promise; a re-synchronisation that truncates entries whose placeholders are still queued therefore shifts the index later reported, and the retained runs show the shift (index 4 while entry 33 sits at 2, or 3 while it sits at 1, the returned index holding entry 35) exactly in the configurations where the origin's two entries were still in its batch at the truncation, and no shift where the flush deadline had already removed them (two-tick deadline at 1000 or 100 ms) or where it fired before the re-synchronisation (100 and 250 ms ticks with the default hundred-tick deadline). That the returned index is supposed to identify the caller's entry is not my reading alone: the example documents the call as confirming durability of a specific entry 'decided at its assigned index' and announces the index without reading the log, and two of the runtime scenarios assert the value at the returned index - one refills an index after a lost entry and asserts the index holds the refill, the other asserts each returned index holds the entry submitted to it, with the message 'wrong-index assignment -- indices are 1-based'. Both mechanisms therefore contradict an expectation the repository itself encodes, and both end in the same caller-visible shape. A value check in `drain_notifiers` would mask both symptoms without fixing either cause: the arm order and the placeholder accounting are independent defects.\",\n   \"counterevidence\": [\n    \"The entry-point documentation promises only that the future resolves when the assigned log index is decided, and advises re-reading the returned index only for `Superseded`; read that way `Ok(idx)` claims nothing about the value at `idx`. That is weaker than what the example and the two asserting test scenarios expect, which is why the finding rests on those expectations rather than on the wording alone.\",\n    \"A new leader must adopt any value a majority-overlapping write quorum already accepted, so an entry that reached a write quorum cannot be replaced; both mechanisms therefore need the assignment to be local-only or the entries to be discarded, which the receipts support (origin decided index 0 in the violating runs, the new leader's decided values excluding the origin's entries).\",\n    \"No retained run exhibits the two mechanisms together in one call, nor a re-synchronisation that both completes and carries decided progress while the origin's entries are discarded, so the intermediate steps of the second mechanism remain unobserved even though its endpoints are complete and reproduced.\",\n    \"The placeholder queue is unobservable through the public API, so its accounting is the explanation the endpoints imply rather than a direct measurement; the flush-deferral and accept-sync rules behind the condition are source-derived and consistent with the phase fields of the runs.\"\n   ],\n   \"limitations\": [\n    \"Six retained configurations, all with batch_size 3 at the origin, a three-node cluster, majority quorum, a leader origin and an in-process in-memory transport; follower origins, other batch sizes, flexible quorums and crash recovery are unmeasured.\",\n    \"The boundary between the correct and shifted outcomes is bracketed rather than located (250 vs 500 ms ticks, and the flush deadline varied only at 100 and 1000 ms).\",\n    \"Both mechanisms are established for this snapshot's build with the default features through the actor layer's public handle; nothing here extends to other revisions of the crate, and the repository's own test scenarios that assert the value at the returned index were not executed here, so whether they pass or fail under these configurations is unknown.\"\n   ]\n  }\n ]\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
